# Dabz System settings
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   1. Make a copy of it named .env.local  (cp .env.example .env.local)
#   2. Paste your real Supabase values into the copy
#   3. Never commit .env.local - it is ignored by git on purpose
#
# Where these come from: Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> API.
# Full walkthrough in docs/SETUP.md.

# The address of your Supabase project. Looks like https://abcdefgh.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The PUBLIC key (labelled "anon" or "publishable"). It is safe in the browser:
# Row Level Security in the database decides what each person may see.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The SECRET key (labelled "service_role"). It bypasses all security, so it
# must NEVER appear in a browser. Note there is no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix here -
# that prefix is what would send a value to the browser, so leaving it off is
# what keeps this key on the server.
#
# Signing in needs it, because looking up which account a username belongs to
# and counting failed attempts both happen before anyone is signed in. Creating
# accounts and resetting passwords need it too.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notifications (Phase 11). Optional: leave these blank and the Settings screen
# simply says notifications are not set up yet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Run `npm run push:keys` once to generate BOTH of these, then paste them here
# and into Vercel. Generating them again invalidates every phone already
# turned on, so do it once and keep them.

# The PUBLIC half. It is handed to every browser that subscribes, which is what
# it is for - the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is correct and deliberate here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=

# The SECRET half. Anyone holding it can send notifications that look like they
# came from your shop. No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, for the same reason as the
# service-role key above.
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# A password the daily-digest cron must present. Vercel sets this header
# automatically for its own cron calls once the variable exists.
#
# Without it the digest route refuses EVERYBODY, including Vercel - failing
# shut on purpose, because a missing secret means nobody configured this, and a
# URL that reads the whole shop must not be left open. Any long random string
# will do: `openssl rand -base64 32`.
CRON_SECRET=
